        "M-O s.v. electric (“passim”)",
        "Traupman 123: <span lang=\"la\">ēlectricum</span>, an electrical appliance",
        "Traupman 166",
        "Traupman 308"
      ],
      "comment": "The modern word, from <span lang=\"la\">ēlectrum</span>, amber (Greek ἤλεκτρον), which draws light things to it when rubbed. It is the word throughout the lexicon: <span lang=\"la\">lūmen ēlectricum, fūniculus ēlectricus, autoraeda ēlectrica</span>. Morgan–Owens asks why the form is not <span lang=\"la\">ēlectrinus</span>, after Greek ἠλέκτρινος, but <span lang=\"la\">ēlectricus</span> is the one in use."
    }
